# {{ project_name }} architecture

This document describes how the generated pipeline moves data from Open-Meteo to the
dbt marts, why the layers are shaped this way, and how to extend the design.

## Data flow

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    API[Open-Meteo API] -->|extract| RAW[data/raw/city/date.json]
    RAW -->|load, delete then insert| DB[(raw.hourly_weather)]
    SEED[dbt seed: cities] --> STG
    DB --> STG[stg_hourly_weather]
    STG --> DAILY[daily_city_weather]
    STG --> EXTREMES[city_extremes]
    DAILY --> EXTREMES
```

- **Extract** makes one request per city, validates the response, and writes one raw
  JSON file per city per UTC date under `data/raw/<city-slug>/<YYYY-MM-DD>.json`.
  Hourly arrays are split by the date in their `time` values. The raw file also records
  the city name, coordinates and units.
- **Load** reads every raw file, deletes the existing rows for that city and date, and
  inserts the file's rows in one transaction into `raw.hourly_weather`.
- **The dbt seed** writes the `cities` seed so marts can be joined to the configured
  city list and tested with `relationships`.
- **Staging** casts the raw columns, drops rows observed after `now()`, and dedupes on
  `(city, observed_at)` keeping the latest `loaded_at`.
- **Marts** aggregate the staging view into daily city statistics and all-time city
  extremes. Both are materialised as tables.

The raw table is:

```text
raw.hourly_weather(
    city,
    observed_at,
    temperature_c,
    humidity_pct,
    precipitation_mm,
    wind_kmh,
    loaded_at
)
```

